---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import NameLists from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/NameLists.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import Register from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Register.astro";
import reg from "../data/register.json";
import nf from "../data/namefold.json";
import pjReg from "../data/people.json";
/* COUNTED, BECAUSE I TYPED IT AN HOUR AGO. The sentence below said «of 350
   people it holds, not one is living». That was correct when written and it
   was a typed cardinal in prose — the same fault as the footer it was written
   to fix, committed while writing about that fault. The D'Arcy session did the
   identical thing in the identical hour, typing «six are named on purpose»
   into its own corrected footer. A number that is right today and asserted is
   a number that goes wrong silently. */
const nPeopleReg = pjReg.people.length;
const nLivingReg = pjReg.people.filter(
  (p) => p.living === true || String(p.conf || "").toLowerCase() === "living").length;
/* THE SEARCH BOX FOLDS SPELLINGS. Without this, a reader typing the surname
   this archive answers to reached 3,504 of the 4,244 times it appears in these
   rows and never learned the other 740 existed - Booijsen, Booysen, Booyzens,
   Booizen. It is worse on given names: eleven Afrikaans/English doublets run to
   8,931 occurrences here, of which the English form is 1,783, so JOHN BOOYZEN
   found a fifth of what is on this page. Folding is a search rule only. It
   widens what the query matches; it merges nothing. */
const rows = reg.rows;
const linked = rows.filter(r => r.u).length;
const groups = reg.groups;
const kindLabel = { index: "surname index", read: "reconstructed", tree: "family tree" };
const byGroup = {};
for (const r of rows) (byGroup[r.g] ||= []).push(r);
const capped = Object.entries(reg.caps || {}).filter(([, c]) => c.got < c.total);

/* The content-visibility this page put on its fourteen sections, and the
   debounce on its search, are in the shared table now - per block of 200 rows
   rather than per surname, so they survive the sections becoming a facet. What
   this archive knew and the others did not is that k is worth filtering on: a
   surname index pulled whole is not a household somebody reconstructed, and
   most of this page is not this family. */
const regRows = rows.map((r) => ({
  name: r.n, surname: r.g,
  href: r.p ? "/who/" + r.p + "/" : "",
  kind: kindLabel[r.k] || r.k,
  chip: r.k === "read" ? "reconstructed" : "",
  detail: r.w,
  source: r.s,
  sourceHref: r.u || "",
  sourceLabel: r.u ? "record" : "no public link",
}));
---
<Base title="The Register"
      eyebrow="Every named individual this archive has found"
      dek={`${rows.length.toLocaleString()} entries for ${new Set(rows.map(r =>
 r.n)).size.toLocaleString()} distinct names, ${linked.toLocaleString()} of them with a link to the source that names them. This is the raw catch — not a family tree, and most of it is not this family.`}>

<NameLists here="register" u={u} />


<section class="blk">
  <h2>Read this before you read the list</h2>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p><b>Most of these entries come from surname indexes.</b> They are every BOOYZEN, MOUNTJOY,
      DASCHNER and the rest that FamilySearch's South African collections hold, pulled a whole surname
      at a time. <b>A shared surname is not a relationship.</b> The Booysens of the northern Cape and
      the Booyzens of Marico are, on the evidence this archive has, largely other people's families.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:12px">Entries marked <span class="chip doc">reconstructed</span> are the people
      this archive has actually joined into households from the words of a record, or met while reading a
      document line by line — the eight children on the 1876 Sterfkennis, the eleven Daschners of 1942,
      the eleven heirs of Barkly East in 1938. Those names link to a person page. Everything else links
      out to the source and is offered as <b>a lead, not a conclusion</b>.</p>
    {/* THIS SENTENCE PROMISED A STRICTER RULE THAN THE ESTATE KEEPS, which is
        the direction nobody checks because it reads as reassurance. It said
        «the living are not here, as everywhere else in this archive» —
        absence — while /about says living people are NAMED AND NOTHING MORE,
        which permits presence. Both cannot describe one regime.

        Prompted by the D'Arcy session, whose /about claimed the living were
        «absent from the build» while six are named deliberately and were
        listed honestly in its own living.json all along. The sentence had
        been written from the rule and never read against the data beside it.

        Read against this archive's data on 21 September: 350 people, NONE
        flagged living, and one born inside eighty years who has a death
        recorded. So the register holds no living person — which is a fact
        about this family, not a policy, and the sentence now says which. */}
    <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>No living person is in this register</b>, and none is
      anywhere in this archive: of the {nPeopleReg} people it holds, {nLivingReg === 0
      ? "not one is living" : nLivingReg + " are living"}. The estate's
      rule, where it has to be applied, is that <b>a living person is named and nothing
      more</b> — no dates, no places, no photographs. Here it has not yet had to be
      applied at all.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What was harvested, and what was left alone</h2>
  <p class="dek">Every count below is the number of people FamilySearch's South African collections hold
    under that spelling. They are worth reading as figures in their own right.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:11rem">Spelling</th><th class="m" style="width:9rem">In the records</th><th class="m" style="width:9rem">Taken</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {Object.entries(reg.caps).map(([name, c]) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="m"><strong>{name.toUpperCase()}</strong></td>
            <td class="m">{c.total.toLocaleString()}</td>
            <td class="m">{c.got ? c.got.toLocaleString() : "—"}</td>
            <td>{c.total === 0
                  ? <span><b>Nothing. Not one record in the country.</b></span>
                  : c.got === 0
                    ? <span><b>Not harvested.</b> Too common to be evidence — a name this frequent would
                        fill this page without telling anyone anything, and an arbitrary few hundred out
                        of it would be worse than nothing.</span>
                    : c.got >= c.total
                      ? <span>Complete.</span>
                      : <span><b>Capped.</b> The index stops paging past this point.</span>}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:18px">
    <b>Three of those rows are findings, not housekeeping.</b>
    <b>BOOYZEN returns 1,350 people in the whole country and BOOYSEN returns 40,592</b> — thirty times as
    many — which is the scale behind everything this archive says about
    <a href={u("/name/")}>the name</a>.
    The eight in-law surnames added on 13 September &mdash; Keys, Buitendag, Dormehl, Siebert, Roodt,
    Olivier, Grobelaar, Palm &mdash; were all tested and all left out for the same reason. <b>OLIVIER
    alone returns 81,327 people in this country</b>, and four hundred of them chosen by a search
    engine's idea of relevance is not a sample of anything.
    And <b>SCHLEHER returns nothing at all</b>, against 851 for <em>Slier</em> and 7 for <em>Sleer</em>:
    the family tree's spelling of Anna Catharina's surname is the one form of it that no South African
    record uses. <a href={u("/women/")}>The women &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Filter</h2>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <Register rows={regRows} u={u} unit="entries" fold={nf.fold} />
</section>



</Base>
